              "text": "...**those owned by wealthy rulers.\"**\n\nThis famous observation is attributed to the 14th-century North African scholar, historian, and philosopher **Ibn Khaldun** (1332\u20131406), from his seminal 1377 work, ***The Muqaddimah*** (also known as the *Prolegomena*). \n\n### Context and Meaning\nIn *The Muqaddimah*, Ibn Khaldun laid down foundational principles of sociology, historiography, and economics. His quote highlights a core principle of early free-market theory:\n\n* **The Inefficiency of State Monopolies:** Ibn Khaldun argued that when rulers or governments try to engage directly in commerce, they inevitably use their political and military power to crowd out private competition, manipulate prices, and force transactions. This stifles the economy, destroys the motivation of everyday citizens to produce wealth, and ultimately collapses the state's tax base.\n* **The Power of the Private Sector:** He believed that true economic prosperity is driven by \"responsible and organized\" private merchants. Because private business owners must carefully manage risk, organize supply chains, and satisfy customers without the crutch of political power, their enterprises are far more resilient, efficient, and productive over the long run.\n\nBecause of insights like this, many modern economists consider Ibn Khaldun to be a major forerunner of modern economic thought and free-market capitalism, prefiguring ideas that Adam Smith would popularize nearly 400 years later.",
